Package: grd-with-usb-redirect
Architecture: all
Depends: rdp-usb-gw,
         gnome-remote-desktop-urbdrc,
         grd-headless-session (= ${source:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: grd-no-lockscreen (= ${source:Version})
Description: GNOME remote desktop with USB redirection, for RDP gateways
 Installs everything for persistent headless GNOME desktops that RDP clients
 reach directly, such as through an RDP gateway, with the client's USB
 devices redirected to the desktop:
  - rdp-usb-gw, the USB redirection daemon
  - a gnome-remote-desktop build that lends rdp-usb-gw the session's USB
    redirection channel
  - grd-headless-session, to start a user's session at boot
  - grd-no-lockscreen, to turn off GNOME's lock screen
 .
 It also pins this PPA above the Ubuntu archive, so an Ubuntu update doesn't
 replace the patched gdm3 and gnome-remote-desktop builds before the PPA is
 rebuilt on it.

Package: grd-headless-session
Architecture: all
Depends: gnome-remote-desktop,
         gnome-remote-desktop (>= 47~) | grdctl-credentials-stdin,
         gdm3 (>= 50~) | gdm-new-session,
         openssl,
         passwd,
         systemd,
         util-linux,
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: persistent headless GNOME sessions reachable over RDP
 grd-headless-session sets up a user's headless GNOME session to start at
 boot and keep running when the RDP client disconnects, like a Windows Remote
 Desktop session. RDP clients connect straight to it with gnome-remote-desktop,
 without going through a login screen.
 .
 Each session gets a self-signed TLS certificate for the machine's name,
 renewed when it nears expiry or the name changes.

Package: grd-no-lockscreen
Architecture: all
Depends: dconf-cli, ${misc:Depends}
Description: turn off GNOME's lock screen for remote sessions
 Sets and locks GNOME's settings so sessions never lock. For desktops reached
 through an RDP login, where the lock screen would be a second password
 prompt, and in a headless session there is no console to unlock it from.
 .
 This applies to every user on the machine.
